## 6. Feature D — Linux-Based Network Emulation & Benchmarking

### 6.1 Purpose

Independently measure network behaviour under configurable conditions, and serve as the controlled experimental apparatus for Features A and C. Emulation is performed by the **Linux kernel's `tc`/`netem` queue discipline**, the standard tool for protocol testing under emulated real-world conditions.

### 6.2 Functional Requirements — Standalone Benchmarking

- Run scenarios on a fixed topology with no self-healing controller requirement.
- Measure RTT/latency, throughput, message/datagram loss, jitter, and run duration.
- Define and document measurement semantics for each metric.
- Store scenario configuration, software version, host details, and raw results in PostgreSQL.
- Generate comparison reports and CSV exports; report median and p95 for repeated runs.
- Label synthetic conditions clearly; never present emulated values as real-world measurements.

### 6.3 Functional Requirements — Linux `tc`/`netem` Emulation

Applied to each path's virtual interface via `tc qdisc`:

| Condition | `tc`/`netem` parameter | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Delay | `delay TIME [JITTER [CORRELATION]]` | Milliseconds; supports uniform, normal, Pareto distributions |
| Jitter | `delay TIME JITTER` | Correlation parameter controls how much previous delay impacts current value |
| Message/datagram loss | `loss random PERCENT [CORRELATION]` | Supports state models and Gilbert-Elliot for correlated loss |
| Duplication | `duplicate PERCENT [CORRELATION]` | Optional; useful for stress testing |
| Packet corruption | `corrupt PERCENT [CORRELATION]` | Optional; simulates bit errors |
| Reordering | `reorder PERCENT [CORRELATION] [gap DISTANCE]` | Simulates out-of-order delivery |
| Rate limit | `rate RATE [PACKETOVERHEAD [CELLSIZE [CELLOVERHEAD]]]` | Token bucket rate limiting |
| Queue depth | `limit COUNT` | Maximum packets held when doing delay |
| Seed | `seed VALUE` | Deterministic emulation for reproducibility |

- All emulation parameters are per-path and configurable per run.
- Emulation must be deterministic when a fixed seed is supplied.
- Emulation configuration is applied and verified before each run; a hash of the configuration is stored with the run record.

### 6.4 Functional Requirements — Apparatus for Feature A

- Fault injection primitives: kill a node process, drop a link (`tc qdisc del`), delay a link, rate-limit a link.
- **Hysteresis sweep harness**: run the same recovery scenario with `hysteresis_ratio ∈ {1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0}` and collect `(recovery_time, route_flap_count)` pairs.
- Emit a Pareto frontier plot of stability versus recovery time.

### 6.5 Functional Requirements — Apparatus for Feature C

- Path heterogeneity configuration: set per-path delay, bandwidth, and loss independently via `tc`/`netem`.
- **Scheduler comparison harness**: run the same transfer under the same path configuration with each scheduler variant (DB-DSP, RR, WRR, single-path, naïve delay-aware) and collect comparison metrics.
- Comparison tables and plots: transfer time, goodput, out-of-order count, retransmission count, per-path utilisation.

### 6.6 Measurement Semantics

- Distinguish **application-message loss** from **datagram loss**; TCP retransmissions can hide packet loss.
- Record the measurement point for every metric (application layer, socket layer, kernel `tc` statistics).
- Document that all conditions are emulated via `tc`/`netem` and that emulated loss/delay approximates but does not perfectly reproduce real NIC/switch behaviour.

### 6.7 Acceptance Criteria

- Users can repeat the same scenario and compare runs with the same configuration.
- Reports include units, workload, repetition count, environment, and limitations.
- Benchmarking works with fixed routes and without the other feature modules.
- The hysteresis sweep harness produces a documented trade-off curve.
- The scheduler comparison harness produces a complete comparison table for all variants.
- Emulation is reproducible given the same seed.
- `tc`/`netem` configuration is verified and logged for every run.

