## 9. Security Requirements

| Threat | Required control | Verification |
|---|---|---|
| Unknown node joins | Certificate-based identity and mutual authentication | Connection rejection test |
| Forged heartbeat | HMAC or protected TLS channel | Invalid MAC rejected |
| Replay of old heartbeat | Sequence number + freshness window | Stale message rejected |
| Replay of stale route update | Route versioning + authenticated channel | Stale route rejected |
| Unauthorised route change | Authenticated API, role checks, audit event | Negative authorisation tests |
| Parameter tampering | Signed run configs; hysteresis/emulation changes audited | Configuration integrity test |
| `tc`/`netem` injection | Emulation config requires operator role; applied configuration logged and hashed | Negative authorisation test; config audit |
| Resource exhaustion | Payload limits, rate limits, bounded queues/retries, timeouts | Malformed and load tests |
| Secret leakage | Secrets out of source and logs; rotation process | Secret scan and log review |
| Vulnerable dependency | Pinned dependencies; regular `pip-audit` | Scan report reviewed |
| Controller/DB outage | Last-known valid state; visible degraded mode | Disconnect tests |

**Security rules**
1. Do not implement custom encryption; use standard TLS libraries. HMAC provides authenticity/integrity, not confidentiality.
2. Do not trust a node merely because its IP is reachable.
3. Separate node, operator, and read-only dashboard identities; least-privilege DB roles.
4. Audit security events without logging secrets or private keys.
5. Security scanners are not proof of security; test runtime behaviour and review findings.
6. Bind control endpoints to loopback by default.
7. `tc`/`netem` commands are executed with least privilege; do not run node processes as root unless required for `tc` configuration.

