## 14. Release Acceptance Checklist

- All three features can run independently via documented command or dashboard.
- Each feature has tests, a reproducible scenario, and measurable output.
- Recovery is claimed only after destination-side delivery is verified.
- Hysteresis produces a documented trade-off curve between recovery time and route flap count.
- File transfer verifies SHA-256 and reports bounded failure when retries are exhausted.
- DB-DSP is compared against round-robin, weighted round-robin, naïve delay-aware, and single-path under heterogeneous paths.
- RTX-CL is compared against RTX-CWND under mixed loss conditions.
- Emulation accuracy is validated against configured `tc`/`netem` delay/loss/rate values.
- Benchmark reports include units, conditions, repetitions, environment, and raw data.
- PostgreSQL stores all run configuration, metrics, and audit events.
- Docker Compose can bring up PostgreSQL + API + dashboard reproducibly.
- Unknown nodes and unauthorised controls are rejected; logs contain no secrets.
- Security scan findings are reviewed and unresolved high-severity items documented.
- Controller/database outage behaviour is explicit and tested.
- Documentation calls this a software overlay prototype and states production limitations.
- Demo metrics come from the running implementation, not fabricated values.
- No third-party APIs, cloud services, or telemetry are used.
- Linux `tc`/`netem` is required and documented as a dependency for Features C and D.
- No custom encryption, no custom scheduler beyond documented DB-CMT algorithms, no undocumented modifications to published algorithms.

